WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:03.580
INTRO:
Dubai is often viewed as one of the bougiest

00:00:03.580 --> 00:00:08.620
areas in the world. From the Burj Khalifa
and the Burj Al Arab to the Atlantis and the

00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:14.790
dozens of lavish malls, it seems like Dubai
is one the best places in the world to live.

00:00:14.790 --> 00:00:19.369
Combine this with no income tax, and it’s
not surprising why so many rich individuals

00:00:19.369 --> 00:00:24.910
decide to retire in Dubai. With that being
said though, Dubai often hogs the global spotlight

00:00:24.910 --> 00:00:29.849
when it comes to the UAE, and people often
overlook the emirate right next to Dubai,

00:00:29.849 --> 00:00:35.230
Abu Dhabi. While Abu Dhabi isn’t talked
about nearly as often, they’re just as well

00:00:35.230 --> 00:00:40.360
off if not more well off, and one of their
biggest flexes is their extremely wealthy

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:46.560
sovereign wealth fund. Abu Dhabi’s wealth
fund currently manages $697 billion. This

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:50.450
isn’t quite the biggest in the world as
there’s three other countries with even

00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:56.040
larger funds. However, the thing note is that
the countries with larger funds have significantly

00:00:56.040 --> 00:01:01.620
larger populations as well. The most recent
population numbers from Abu Dhabi indicate

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:08.760
that there’s 1.54 million residents in the
city. If we divide $697 billion by 1.54 million

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:17.670
people, we get a whopping $452,597 per person,
but it gets even better than that. You see,

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:21.930
while all residents of the city directly or
indirectly benefit from the sovereign wealth

00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:27.540
fund, the fund is primarily meant for Abu
Dhabi citizens. And as we know, most of the

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:34.620
people in the UAE aren’t actually citizens.
It’s estimated that only 11.48% of the UAE’s

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:40.800
population are nationals of the country while
88.52% are expats. This number is likely skewed

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:46.229
by Dubai, but even if we assume that 20% of
Abu Dhabi’s residents are nationals, we

00:01:46.229 --> 00:01:53.430
only get 308,000 people. And if we divide
$697 billion by 308,000 people, we get a cool

00:01:53.430 --> 00:02:00.050
$2.262 million per citizen. To put that in
perspective, if we divide social securities’

00:02:00.050 --> 00:02:08.330
$2.852 trillion in assets by the 291 million
citizens in the country, we get um $9,774

00:02:08.330 --> 00:02:13.810
per citizen. But before you try to move to
Abu Dhabi, unless you’re from a neighboring

00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:19.730
country, you won't be able to wriggle yourself
anytime soon. The UAE has extremely strict

00:02:19.730 --> 00:02:24.040
citizenship policies, and you’ll need to
live in the UAE for at least 30 years to even

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:29.510
apply. So, while you and I probably can’t
benefit from this, it's insane to see how

00:02:29.510 --> 00:02:34.770
well off Abu Dhabi citizens really are. Just
a hundred years ago though, Abu Dhabi was

00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:38.421
a no name country that was filled with nomads
who were just trying to fight for survival

00:02:38.421 --> 00:02:43.080
on a daily basis. So, here’s how the average
Abu Dhabi national went from being nomads

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:46.930
to being multi millionaires within just a
few generations.

00:02:46.930 --> 00:02:53.930
STRIKING LIQUID GOLD:
Taking a look back, the story of Abu Dhabi

00:02:53.930 --> 00:03:00.970
only dates back to 1761. Before 1761, there’s
no records of human civilization in the town

00:03:00.970 --> 00:03:05.319
specifically, which makes sense given that
the area was just a desert which is extremely

00:03:05.319 --> 00:03:10.300
difficult to inhabit without the help of modern
technology. Nonetheless, a few tribesmen from

00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:16.260
the Al Bu Falah clan decided to set up a small
town in Abu Dhabi in 1761. Originally, the

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:21.050
area was called “Milh” which means salt
in Arabic. One of the driving factors of the

00:03:21.050 --> 00:03:25.349
early Abu Dhabi economy was pearl diving.
The tribesmen would trade these pearls with

00:03:25.349 --> 00:03:29.769
the Iranians and the Indians. This provided
them with enough capital to sustain a small

00:03:29.769 --> 00:03:35.240
civilization, but Abu Dhabi’s population
never exceeded a few thousand people. In fact,

00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:40.540
even at the start of 1900, Abu Dhabi only
had a total of 6000 residents. Despite the

00:03:40.540 --> 00:03:45.910
small population, Abu Dhabi would soon struggle
to even support these residents. You see,

00:03:45.910 --> 00:03:50.400
throughout the early 1900s, Japan’s pearling
industry would take off which led to Abu Dhabi

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:55.780
losing most of their customers. To make things
worse, the great depression would hit in 1929

00:03:55.780 --> 00:04:00.840
which wiped out the few remaining customers
Abu Dhabi had. In desperation, residents diverted

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:06.260
their focus onto camel herding and the production
of dates and vegetables, but things were quite

00:04:06.260 --> 00:04:10.000
rough. Most residents lived in huts built
from palm leaves while wealthier residents

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:14.940
lived in mud brick homes. But, soon enough
the country would get one glimpse of hope.

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:20.850
On March 3, 1938, Saudi Arabia would not only
discover oil but one of the largest oil reserves

00:04:20.850 --> 00:04:25.840
in the world. Abu Dhabi’s ruler at the time,
Sheikh Shakbut would instantly authorize foreign

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:30.770
companies to come in and start looking for
oil in 1939, but this turned out to be an

00:04:30.770 --> 00:04:35.660
extremely long process. You see, World War
2 would really pick up steam in the first

00:04:35.660 --> 00:04:41.330
half of the 1940s which put oil exploration
on hold. Even after World War 2 though, the

00:04:41.330 --> 00:04:45.410
search for oil was rather difficult due to
the large sand dunes and sand storms all across

00:04:45.410 --> 00:04:51.940
Abu Dhabi. Also, the oil companies were likely
not that motivated to find oil in Abu Dhabi.

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:56.260
After all, Abu Dhabi was a rather small area
and they had already found an ocean of oil

00:04:56.260 --> 00:05:02.470
in Saudi Arabia. So, they had little need
to find oil in Abu Dhabi. Nonetheless, they

00:05:02.470 --> 00:05:08.020
kept looking, and the first sign of discovery
would come in 1951. Light traces of oil and

00:05:08.020 --> 00:05:13.250
gas would be discovered at the Murban No.1
well at a depth of 10,000 ft. Engineers would

00:05:13.250 --> 00:05:18.540
enthusiastically continue drilling deeper,
but at 12,500 ft they would make an unpleasant

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:24.740
discovery: sour gas. Sour gas is a natural
gas that’s extremely poisonous and flammable.

00:05:24.740 --> 00:05:28.540
One petroleum engineer would unfortunately
be killed when sour gas burst out from the

00:05:28.540 --> 00:05:34.020
pipes. For obvious reasons, drilling at this
site was halted, but the search for oil was

00:05:34.020 --> 00:05:40.450
not. And eventually, in 1958, Abu Dhabi would
finally discover oil at the Umm Shaif field

00:05:40.450 --> 00:05:45.130
nearly 20 years after the search had begun.

00:05:45.130 --> 00:05:50.070
INDUSTRIALIZATION:
While Abu Dhabi had struck oil, oil money

00:05:50.070 --> 00:05:55.410
didn’t change the emirate all that much.
You see, Sheikh Shakbut was extremely cautious

00:05:55.410 --> 00:06:00.850
of spending the oil money because he didn’t
know how long it would last. It took 19 years

00:06:00.850 --> 00:06:06.060
to find a viable oil field, and if this field
ran dry, it could take another 19 years or

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:11.280
even more to find another. So, while Sheikh
Shakbut spent some of the money on concrete

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:16.800
buildings and paved roads, he saved the vast
majority of it. But as years passed and Abu

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:20.919
Dhabi was able to negotiate better profit
sharing agreements, much of the government

00:06:20.919 --> 00:06:25.889
and the people felt that Sheikh Shakbut could
be less frugal. These tensions eventually

00:06:25.889 --> 00:06:30.580
blew over in 1966 when Sheikh Shakbut was
basically forced to retire and replaced by

00:06:30.580 --> 00:06:35.680
his brother Zayed. Unlike Sheikh Shakbut,
Sheikh Zayed was extremely optimistic and

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:40.291
felt that the oil money could revolutionize
Abu Dhabi forever. But, he decided to start

00:06:40.291 --> 00:06:45.160
simple and establish the basic necessities
of a city. He went ahead and started building

00:06:45.160 --> 00:06:48.880
a road network that connected the center of
the city with the various villages around

00:06:48.880 --> 00:06:53.110
the city. He also built a seawall on the north
side of the city which protected the city

00:06:53.110 --> 00:06:57.919
from future damage from the ocean. He followed
up these constructions with electricity, running

00:06:57.919 --> 00:07:02.670
water, and a central sewage system. Once the
necessities were covered, he shifted his focus

00:07:02.670 --> 00:07:07.229
onto building modern government buildings,
hotels, and housing projects. Around the same

00:07:07.229 --> 00:07:10.930
time, Britain would also leave the Persian
Gulf which allowed for the United Arab Emirates

00:07:10.930 --> 00:07:15.550
to gain political independence. Soon after,
Abu Dhabi would be made the provisional capital

00:07:15.550 --> 00:07:21.010
of the UAE which made Sheikh Zayed the president
of the entire country. Within just 10 years,

00:07:21.010 --> 00:07:26.199
Abu Dhabi went from being a city of nomads
to gaining independence and rapidly industrializing.

00:07:26.199 --> 00:07:31.039
But Sheikh Zayed was careful to not let this
progress get to his head. Sheikh Zayed was

00:07:31.039 --> 00:07:35.259
also well aware that the oil money could stop
at any point and that eventually the world

00:07:35.259 --> 00:07:39.580
would move towards more sustainable forms
of energy. So, he started investing excess

00:07:39.580 --> 00:07:45.430
oil revenue as soon as possible. Initially,
in 1967, he created the Financial Investments

00:07:45.430 --> 00:07:49.910
Board which was responsible for investing
the excess oil revenue. This was quite an

00:07:49.910 --> 00:07:55.690
ambitious move for the time. You see, back
then and even now, countries mainly invest

00:07:55.690 --> 00:08:00.919
their money into gold and bonds. So, to create
an investing body that would make investments

00:08:00.919 --> 00:08:06.110
outside these categories was quite unique.
This investment body didn’t last long though.

00:08:06.110 --> 00:08:10.940
Sheikh Zayed soon realized that for such a
system to truly be effective, it had to distanced

00:08:10.940 --> 00:08:16.599
from the government as much as possible. So,
in 1976, Sheikh Zayed founded a new organization

00:08:16.599 --> 00:08:21.940
called the Abu Dhabi Investment Authority
or the ADIA. As you would guess, he transferred

00:08:21.940 --> 00:08:26.349
the responsibility of investing excess oil
revenue over to them, and this marks the beginning

00:08:26.349 --> 00:08:29.680
of Abu Dhabi’s sovereign wealth fund.

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:33.800
ADIA:
Ever since the fund was founded, they have

00:08:33.800 --> 00:08:38.539
slowly invested into basically everything
you can think of. This includes equities,

00:08:38.539 --> 00:08:43.689
fixed income, treasuries, real estate, private
equity, and hedge funds. Abu Dhabi doesn’t

00:08:43.689 --> 00:08:48.209
release a full on report of all of their holdings
like Norway, but they do release a breakdown

00:08:48.209 --> 00:08:52.749
of the categories in which they’re invested.
Taking a look at the breakdown, we can see

00:08:52.749 --> 00:08:57.410
that their biggest investment is in developed
equities where they hold between 32 and 42%

00:08:57.410 --> 00:09:01.959
of their money. They also hold a decent amount
in emerging market equities and government

00:09:01.959 --> 00:09:06.519
bonds. Their smallest category, however, is
small cap equities where they only invest

00:09:06.519 --> 00:09:11.999
1 to 5% of their money. As you can see, the
ADIA’s holdings are pretty well diversified

00:09:11.999 --> 00:09:17.529
and they avoid risky investments as much as
possible, but they’re not prone to making

00:09:17.529 --> 00:09:21.179
mistakes. Over the decades, they’ve made
a handful of painful mistakes that have cost

00:09:21.179 --> 00:09:25.819
them hundreds of millions and sometimes even
billions. One of these mistakes was getting

00:09:25.819 --> 00:09:31.160
involved in the Bank of Credit and Commerce
International or the BCCI. In the 1980s, the

00:09:31.160 --> 00:09:37.779
BCCI was the 7th largest private bank with
over $20 billion in assets, so it likely seemed

00:09:37.779 --> 00:09:42.569
like a pretty safe place to invest some money.
But, the BCCI would eventually be linked with

00:09:42.569 --> 00:09:46.870
a massive money laundering scheme and loads
of other financial crimes which led to the

00:09:46.870 --> 00:09:52.949
bank closing in 1991 and the ADIA losing hundreds
of millions. Similarly, another major mistake

00:09:52.949 --> 00:09:59.560
the ADIA made was investing heavily in 2007
and 8. The ADIA was literally buying the top

00:09:59.560 --> 00:10:05.600
of the housing bubble, and one of their biggest
investments was $7.5 billion into CitiGroup.

00:10:05.600 --> 00:10:11.230
Once the housing bubble popped though, CitiGroup
stock crashed 90% and it never recovered.

00:10:11.230 --> 00:10:16.489
The ADIA suffered several similar losses during
this time period. But, despite the downfall,

00:10:16.489 --> 00:10:20.290
the ADIA continued to consistently invest
and it’s worked out pretty well for them

00:10:20.290 --> 00:10:26.660
overall. The last time the ADIA revealed annual
returns was in 2010, and at the time, they

00:10:26.660 --> 00:10:32.779
boasted an average annual return of 8.1% over
the past 30 years. So, they basically just

00:10:32.779 --> 00:10:37.639
match market returns, but it should be noted
that the ADIA is likely far more diversified

00:10:37.639 --> 00:10:40.589
than the S&amp;P 500.

00:10:40.589 --> 00:10:44.990
CASHING IN:
At the end of the day, having hundreds of

00:10:44.990 --> 00:10:50.079
billions in the bank is great and all, but
the goal is to eventually cash in at a sustainable

00:10:50.079 --> 00:10:55.049
pace. Abu Dhabi has avoided cashing in as
much as possible because they have much bigger

00:10:55.049 --> 00:11:00.049
ambitions for this fund. They don’t want
the fund to just give their citizens a solid

00:11:00.049 --> 00:11:04.049
retirement, but eventually make up for oil
revenue as the world transitions to sustainable

00:11:04.049 --> 00:11:08.379
energy. This is why one of the top goals of
the fund is to move all of their assets to

00:11:08.379 --> 00:11:14.370
sustainable investments. We don’t know exactly
how much the ADIA has in sustainable investments,

00:11:14.370 --> 00:11:19.799
but some sources suggest that the figure is
around ⅔. So, Abu Dhabi still has some work

00:11:19.799 --> 00:11:24.399
to do, but it looks like they’re headed
in the right direction. With that being said,

00:11:24.399 --> 00:11:30.049
it doesn’t like withdrawals will become
a thing anytime soon. It seems like Abu Dhabi

00:11:30.049 --> 00:11:32.559
really wants to get the fund to trillions
of dollars first, but then again that’s

00:11:32.559 --> 00:11:36.579
exactly what Norway has been doing as well.
So, in the meantime, Abu Dhabi citizens’

00:11:36.579 --> 00:11:41.800
multi millions is just paper wealth, but hey,
it’s still way better than social security.

00:11:41.800 --> 00:11:45.339
Do you guys think more countries should embrace
a sovereign wealth fund? Comment that down

00:11:45.339 --> 00:11:51.220
below. Also, drop a like if you wish you had
a part of this pie. And of course, consider

00:11:51.220 --> 00:11:55.269
checking out our international channels to
watch our videos in other languages and consider

00:11:55.269 --> 00:12:01.040
subscribing to see more questions logically
